This document describes the command-line programs included in this
directory, including makersakey, x509make, x509read.

These programs require Nettle and GNU Multi Precision.

Types of keys other than RSA is not implemented yet. Passworded private
keys are also not implemented yet.


=== makersakey ===

This program is used for making a RSA key pair, written to stdout.

You must invoke this program with the following switches:

-b
  If specified, then the output is binary format. If not specified, then
  it will be encoded as base64, with a PEM header.

-e <number>
  The size of the public exponent, in bits. This is mutually exclusive with
  the -x switch; use only one or the other.

-m <number>
  The size of the modulus, in bits. This switch is mandatory.

-r <file>
  The file or device to read random data from. The default is /dev/urandom
  if you do not specify a different one. If the specified file runs out of
  data, then it is an error.

-x <number>
  Specifies the exact value of the public exponent, in decimal. This is
  mutually exclusive with the -e switch; use only one or the other. The
  default value is 65537, if you do not specify -e or -x.


=== x509make ===

This program will make a X.509 certificate, written to stdout.

-B [c]<number or ->
  Specify the basic constraints, where c means critical and the number is
  the number of further certificates (including the end certificate, so
  zero means the new certificate cannot issue any more certificates) or -
  if there is no limit.

-S <name>
  Specify the type of signature algorithm, according to the hash name. It
  can be "md5", "sha1", "sha256", or "sha512". The default is "sha256".

-T <number>
  Activate a testing mode. Zero is for normal operation (which is the
  default value, so does not need to be specified). Other numbers are not
  documented; see the source code for details if you need this function,
  and note that the meanings may change in future. Using this switch with
  any value other than zero might result in an invalid certificate.

-b
  If specified, then the output is in binary format. If not specified, then
  it will be encoded as base64, with a PEM header.

-c <name>
  Specify the character encoding for command-line arguments. Currently, the
  only possibility is "ascii", which is also the default value.

-i [=]<file>
  Specify the file containing the issuer's certificate. If = is included
  then it is expected to be binary format, otherwise it is base64. Omit
  this to create a self-signed root certificate.

-k [=]<file>
  Specify the file containing the issuer's private key. If = is included
  then it is expected to be binary format, otherwise it is base64. If this
  is omitted, then the new certificate will not have a signature.

-r
  If specified, reads additional data from stdin, which must be DER format.
  This is a key/value list; see below for the list of possible keys.

You can specify key=value pairs on the command-line, in which case they are
treated as text with the character encoding specified by the -c switch. If
the -r switch is specified, then additional key/value pairs are read from
the input and can be of any type rather than only text.

basic =
  The same as the -B switch.

domain =
  Specify the domain names for the new certificate, in the subject
  alternative names extension. This can either be a sequence of strings,
  or a single string with the names separated by commas.

expires =
  The date/time that the certificate expires. If omitted, then it does not
  expire. This can be a date/time or it can be a UTC-based time interval.

ext =
  Define the sequence of extensions. This is in the standard format in
  X.509 but without the explicit type.

extkeyusage =
  A set or sequence of OIDs, or a ASCII string with a combination of any of
  the following separated by commas: tls_server, tls_client, exec, mail,
  timestamp, ocsp, ssh_client, ssh_server, any.

key =
  Define the subject's public key (in the same format that it is found in a
  X.509 certificate). If it is a self-signed certificate, then this is not
  needed and it will convert the private key to a public key.

keyusage =
  Either a bit string, or a ASCII string with a combination of any of the
  following separated by commas: 

name =
  The subject name. This can either be the sequence with the pieces like in
  the X.509 certificate, or a text string. (However, the format as a text
  string is not implemented yet.)

starts =
  The date/time that it starts to be valid. If omitted, then the current
  time is used.

